      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function in different intervals determined by the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign, namely \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\). These points divide the real number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\), \\( [-1, 1] \\), and \\( (1, \\infty) \\).\n\n**Step 1: Analyze the function in each interval.**\n\n1. **For \\( x < -1 \\):**\n   - \\( |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\)\n   - \\( |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\)\n   - Therefore, \\( G(x) = -x-1 - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2 \\).\n\n2. **For \\( -1 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\):**\n   - \\( |x+1| = x+1 \\)\n   - \\( |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\)\n   - Therefore, \\( G(x) = x+1 - (-x+1) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x \\).\n   - As \\( x \\) varies from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\), \\( G(x) \\) varies from \\( 2(-1) = -2 \\) to \\( 2(1) = 2 \\).\n\n3. **For \\( x > 1 \\):**\n   - \\( |x+1| = x+1 \\)\n   - \\( |x-1| = x-1 \\)\n   - Therefore, \\( G(x) = x+1 - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2 \\).\n\n**Step 2: Determine the range.**\nFrom the analysis above, we see that:\n- For \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n- For \\( -1 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\), \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\).\n- For \\( x > 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\nThus, the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is all values from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\), inclusive of both endpoints. In interval notation, this is written as:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-2, 2]}\n\\]",
