      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function by considering the different cases for the value of \\( x \\). The expression \\( |x+1| - |x-1| \\) changes its form depending on the value of \\( x \\).\n\n1. **Case 1: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)**\n   - If \\( x \\geq 1 \\), then \\( |x+1| = x+1 \\) and \\( |x-1| = x-1 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x + 1 - x + 1 = 2 \\).\n   - So, for \\( x \\geq 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\n2. **Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)**\n   - If \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), then \\( |x+1| = x+1 \\) and \\( |x-1| = 1-x \\) (since \\( x-1 \\) is negative).\n   - Therefore, \\( G(x) = (x+1) - (1-x) = x + 1 - 1 + x = 2x \\).\n   - As \\( x \\) varies from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\), \\( G(x) \\) varies from \\(2(-1) = -2\\) to \\(2(1) = 2\\).\n   - So, for \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\).\n\n3. **Case 3: \\( x < -1 \\)**\n   - If \\( x < -1 \\), then \\( |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\) and \\( |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( G(x) = (-x-1) - (-x+1) = -x - 1 + x - 1 = -2 \\).\n   - So, for \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n\nFrom the above cases, we see that the function \\( G(x) \\) can take any value in the interval \\([-2, 2]\\). Therefore, the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is \\(\\boxed{[-2, 2]}\\).",
